      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "[auto-routed: surprise=1.00] Execute the capacity_scan skill for the rigid constraint family to verify zero-violation reachability and measure N_c(w) trend. This involves constructing a low-dimensional linear constraint family (rigid), training two-layer tanh networks with L-BFGS-B across widths 4-64 using 3 random seeds, and checking if the minimum width N_c(w) remains flat or increases as constraint count w grows. The primary goal is to establish whether the rigid family exhibits the expected low-dimensional solution space behavior (non-increasing N_c) or an unexpected increasing trend that contradicts theoretical expectations."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "For the rigid family, N_c(w) will be flat (constant ~4-8 width) across all w values (5,10,15,20,30) because the linear constraint family has a unique analytical solution that small networks can easily capture. Training errors will be ≤1e-4 and validation errors ≤1e-3 at the minimum width. The surprise value of 1.0 from previous iterations suggests this might deviate from expectations, so the actual result may show increasing N_c or higher errors indicating optimization difficulties."
    },
